{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C4QjyeYrw5fU17tBKyvpTT","type":"tool_result","content":"./internal/gitx/inspect_test.go:504:\ta, err := g.PatchID(ctx, dir, c1, c2, \"f.txt\")\n./internal/gitx/inspect_test.go:508:\tif b, err := g.PatchID(ctx, dir, c1, c2, \"f.txt\"); err != nil || a != b {\n./internal/gitx/inspect_test.go:511:\tif b, err := g.PatchID(ctx, dir, c3, c4, \"f.txt\"); err != nil || a == b {\n./internal/gitx/inspect_test.go:514:\tif b, err := g.PatchID(ctx, dir, c4, c5, \"f.txt\"); err != nil || a == b {\n./internal/gitx/inspect_test.go:517:\tif _, err := g.PatchID(ctx, dir, c1, c1, \"f.txt\"); err == nil {\n./internal/gitx/inspect_test.go:520:\tif _, err := g.PatchID(ctx, dir, c1, \"nope\", \"f.txt\"); err == nil {\n./internal/prgate/prgate_test.go:359:type errPatchGit struct {\n./internal/prgate/prgate_test.go:364:func (g errPatchGit) MergeBase(context.Context, string, string, string) (string, error) {\n./internal/prgate/prgate_test.go:367:func (g errPatchGit) Parents(context.Context, string, string) ([]string, error) {\n./internal/prgate/prgate_test.go:370:func (g errPatchGit) PatchID(context.Context, string, string, string, string) (string, error) {\n./internal/prgate/prgate_test.go:374:func TestCoversPatchGitError(t *testing.T) {\n./internal/prgate/prgate_test.go:380:\tfor name, g := range map[string]errPatchGit{\n./internal/prgate/prgate_test.go:398:\tc, err := CoversDetail(context.Background(), errPatchGit{Git: fg, patchErr: boom}, \"\", \"rev\", \"head\", \"\", 1)\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:110:// PatchGit is the part of *gitx.Git CoversDetail adds to Git.\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:111:type PatchGit interface {\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:115:\tPatchID(ctx context.Context, repoDir, from, to, path string) (string, error)\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:130:func CoversDetail(ctx context.Context, g PatchGit, repoDir, reviewed, head, base string, issue int) (Cover, error) {\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:140:func firstUpdateMerge(ctx context.Context, g PatchGit, repoDir, reviewed, head string) (string, error) {\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:158:func cover(ctx context.Context, g Git, pg PatchGit, repoDir, reviewed, head, base string, issue int) (Cover, error) {\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:206:\t\ta, err := pg.PatchID(ctx, repoDir, oldBase, reviewed, p)\n./internal/prgate/prgate.go:210:\t\tb, err := pg.PatchID(ctx, repoDir, base, head, p)\n./internal/merger/loop.go:69:\tPatchID(ctx context.Context, repoDir, from, to, path string) (string, error)\n// directory of the PR's own issue) changed between the two. With base = merge-base(main, head), a changed path is also tolerated\n// when head has the base's version of it, because it came from main (an\n// update-branch merge). A path both the PR and main changed since the review\n// is not covered. Any git error fails closed: the error is returned and the\n// head is not covered.\nfunc Covers(ctx context.Context, g Git, repoDir, reviewed, head, base string, issue int) (bool, []string, error) {\n\tc, err := cover(ctx, g, nil, repoDir, reviewed, head, base, issue)\n\treturn c.OK, c.Broken, err\n}\n\n// PatchGit is the part of *gitx.Git CoversDetail adds to Git.\ntype PatchGit interface {\n\tGit\n\tMergeBase(ctx context.Context, repoDir, a, b string) (string, error)\n\tParents(ctx context.Context, repoDir, rev string) ([]string, error)\n\tPatchID(ctx context.Context, repoDir, from, to, path string) (string, error)\n}\n\n// Cover is the outcome of CoversDetail.\ntype Cover struct {\n\tOK         bool\n\tBroken     []string // paths that break the cover\n\tPatchEqual []string // paths both sides changed whose PR patch is unchanged\n}\n\n// CoversDetail is Covers with one more rule for base != \"\": a path both the PR\n// and main changed is still covered when the PR's own patch to it (stable\n// patch id of the diff from the merge base) is the same before and after the\n// update. That needs head to reach reviewed along first parents through\n// two-parent merges only (update-branch merges). Any git error fails closed.\nfunc CoversDetail(ctx context.Context, g PatchGit, repoDir, reviewed, head, base string, issue int) (Cover, error) {\n\treturn cover(ctx, g, g, repoDir, reviewed, head, base, issue)\n}\n238:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.\n326:**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).\n367:3. **Run the aid, then the agent.** After the pin, a role with an executable `aid` in its directory (reviewers only, like a scanner's `scan`; #205) has it run by the controller as its own executor job at the pinned head, before the agent, so the findings of its deterministic tools do not depend on the host or on the agent choosing to run them. The tool finds; the agent explains, ranks and confirms. The aid job is read-only, has no `Env` and no `Fixed` entries (no model credential, no GitHub token), runs with `--network none` under Docker (a role `Dockerfile` or `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`; known CVEs stay with the `cve` scanner, so the aid never queries vulnerability databases), and has its own timeout of 5 minutes (shorter if the role's is), counted inside the review job's timeout: the agent gets what is left, at least one minute. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/changed.txt` (one path per line, `.ard/<issue#>/` left out) and `diff.patch`. Its output is JSONL on stdout, one object per result with exactly `tool`, `rule`, `file`, `line`, `severity` (`critical`, `high`, `medium`, `low`, `info`) and `message`, capped at 256 KiB (more is a failure). ard validates it: a line that is not JSON, has an unknown key, a bad tool, rule, severity or line, or a path that is absolute, has `..` or is not in `changed.txt` is dropped and counted; a message is redacted, cut at 1 KiB and secret-scanned like a log, and a line that still trips the scanner is dropped. Matched text is never kept: the aid must print rule ids and positions only. The result is written to `.ard-input/<issue#>/aid.jsonl` and is the first of the agent's untrusted inputs; the starter prompt reads it first and runs the tools itself only when the file is absent. **Failure:** a failed, timed-out or invalid aid (any dropped line counts as invalid) never stops the review. The input says the aid failed and why, and close-out turns an `approve` into `changes` with a note. **Blocking:** a `gitleaks` result on a line the PR added becomes a blocking `high` finding with a `[secret]` message that ard adds to the review in code (carrying the rule id only); the agent cannot drop it, and an `approve` becomes `changes`. gosec and semgrep results stay advisory. Both rules apply only to roles that have an `aid`. **Host mode:** with no role `Dockerfile` and no `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`, the aid runs on the host, as ard-owned code that reads but never executes PR code; its environment holds no credential, but nothing enforces its network, and it runs as the controller's uid without a sandbox: the residual risk of #84 applies to it too. The starter `security-review/aid` pins gosec, gitleaks and semgrep (vendored local rules, `--metrics=off`, `--disable-nosem`) in the role's `Dockerfile`, built on the default ard-agent image (k8s role images stay with #245). Then **run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In a later round it also gets `.ard-input/<issue#>/previous-review.md`, its own earlier report, read from the pinned head, and says which findings are resolved.\n373:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55. It also checks that every fetched file has a `fetched:` entry, a `CAPTURE.md` row and a regen target, and re-verifies pinned checksums against the publisher's checksum file when it has network; a capture note is agent-written, so it is not evidence on its own (#301).\n375:**Repo context in the starter reviewers.** `eng-review`, `license-review` and `security-review` read `TECH_STACK.md`; `product-manager-review` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`. Each file is optional and a template-only file counts as absent. A conflict between the change and a file is reported as a finding. The PR-head rule: reviewers trust the files only while `diff.patch` does not touch `.ard/*.md`; if it does, that is a blocking `high` finding and the changed files are not relied on.\n402:- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does, unless the PR's own patch to that path is unchanged: the stable patch id of the diff from the merge base is identical before and after the update, the head reaches the reviewed commit along first parents through two-parent merges only, and no git step failed. A conflict resolution, a hand edit or a later PR commit changes the patch and breaks the cover, and any git error is not covered. The merger logs the paths covered this way as `merge.cover_patch_equal`; the reviewer's mark-ready and reviewfix landing keep the plain rule). A review made stale by code that changed since it is re-requested once per head: the merger removes `ard:review:<role>:done` (the reviewer then claims the role again at the current head), records a `review_rerequest` event for that PR and head, and logs `merge.review_rerequested`. A head that moved only by an update-branch merge from `main` is still covered and triggers nothing. Under dry-run it logs `merge.would_rerequest` and writes only to the ledger, with no event\n564:- **Versioning ([#183](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/183)).** `version.yml` gives every first-parent merge to `main` an annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and a notes-only release, oldest first. The bump comes from the merged PR's labels: `semver:major`, `semver:minor`, else patch; a direct push gets patch, and ard's own main-lane commits (committer `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`) are skipped. The seed tag is `v0.1.0`. The workflow is a dry run until the repository variable `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. `make build` (`git describe` limited to plain `vX.Y.Z` tags) and `go install ...@vX.Y.Z` (build info) both print the tag from `ard version`. `internal/versioncheck` tests the workflow's scripts. See `docs/runbooks/release.md`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c728f002-51cb-4e7c-ab13-963c0e6353ab","uuid":"cd765e32-5ae4-4d6e-a6cc-453328040f69","timestamp":"2026-11-25T08:12:23.375Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"./internal/gitx/inspect_test.go:504:\ta, err := g.PatchID(ctx, dir